    "message": {
      "content": "# Distributed Tensor and Expert Parallel Inference: A Technical Note\n\n## 1. Overview and Motivation\n\nModern large models exceed the memory and compute capacity of a single accelerator. Two complementary parallelism strategies address this during inference:\n\n- **Tensor Parallelism (TP)**: Splits individual weight matrices (and activations) across devices. Every device holds a shard of every layer and processes the *same* tokens.\n- **Expert Parallelism (EP)**: Splits the *experts* of a Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) layer across devices. Each device holds a few complete experts; different tokens are routed to different devices.\n\nIn practice, serving stacks (e.g., DeepSpeed-Inference, Megatron, vLLM, TensorRT-LLM) combine TP for dense layers/attention and EP for MoE layers, often alongside data parallelism (DP) for request-level throughput.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Tensor Parallel Inference\n\n### 2.1 What is sharded\n\nFor a transformer layer:\n\n- **Attention**: Query/Key/Value projection weights are column-parallel (each device computes attention for a slice of heads). The output projection is row-parallel.\n- **MLP**: The up-projection `W1` (and gate `Wg` in SwiGLU) is column-parallel; the down-projection `W2` is row-parallel.\n- **Embeddings / LM head**: Vocabulary-parallel (each device owns a vocab slice) or replicated.\n\n### 2.2 Data flow per layer\n\n```\nInput hidden states x (identical on all TP ranks)\n        \u2502\n        \u251c\u2500\u2500 Column-parallel matmul: Y_i = x \u00b7 W1_i        (no communication)\n        \u2502      each rank i computes its slice of intermediate activations\n        \u251c\u2500\u2500 Nonlinearity applied elementwise locally\n        \u2502\n        \u251c\u2500\u2500 Row-parallel matmul: Z_i = Y_i \u00b7 W2_i",
      "reasoning": "",
      "role": "assistant"
    },
